structural

spec.describe asks a runtime to describe ITSELF. Its deltas include the line
"Cmds (this runtime): ...", which is necessarily different for every
implementation, because implementations support different commands.

Byte-comparing this fixture would therefore require every runtime to recite
the reference runtime's command list — to misreport its own capabilities in
order to score as conforming. That is the opposite of what a conformance suite
should reward, and it penalises a correct implementation for being honest.

The property that genuinely holds across every conforming runtime is the
shape: started, then one or more deltas, then an outcome. That is what is
compared here.
